# Carry or Fall — example environment (M7)
# Copy to `.env` for local development. NEVER put real secrets in this tracked example.
# The copied file runs locally without Supabase. Replace and uncomment all four
# Supabase examples together only when connecting to a real development project.

# ----- Client (Vite). Only VITE_-prefixed vars are exposed to the browser bundle. -----
# WebSocket URL of the local authoritative game server.
VITE_GAME_SERVER_URL=ws://localhost:2567
# Build version the client displays and sends for version checks.
VITE_BUILD_VERSION=0.7.0-boss

# ----- Server -----
NODE_ENV=development
# Port the game server (HTTP health + Colyseus WSS) listens on.
PORT=2567
# Comma-separated allowed browser origins for CORS (technical plan section 20.2).
# For local development this is the Vite dev server origin.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173
# Build version the server reports in health and synchronized state.
GAME_BUILD_VERSION=0.7.0-boss
# Log level: debug | info | warn | error
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ----- Optional: reproducible / accelerated matches -----
# Both are read by the game server only; neither reaches the browser bundle.
# The end-to-end browser suite sets them itself (apps/client/playwright.config.ts),
# so you do not need them here — they are documented because every variable the
# server reads belongs in this file.

# Pins the seed every match is generated from, making enemy, loot, chip, and
# extraction placement reproducible (technical plan section 9.4). Leave unset for
# a fresh random seed per match, which is the normal case.
# MATCH_SEED=76

# Shortens the lobby countdown that runs before a match starts (concept section
# 22.2). Leave unset for the gameplay default, which is sized so a human can join
# a friend's match.
# MATCH_LOBBY_MS=8000

# ----- Supabase: accounts and progression (M5, technical plan section 20.2) -----
#
# All four are OPTIONAL for local development. With none of them set, the server
# runs on an in-memory progression store and the client plays as a guest with no
# account. A fresh clone with no .env passes every gate; copy this file to .env
# before starting the local client (docs/DECISIONS.md D46). NODE_ENV=production without the server pair is a
# startup failure, so a real deployment can never quietly lose every account's
# progress.
#
# Setting them up: see supabase/README.md. Anonymous sign-ins must be enabled in
# the project, and the schema comes from supabase/migrations/ — never from
# hand-editing in the dashboard.

# Client half. The publishable key is DESIGNED to be in the browser bundle: it
# grants nothing on its own, because row-level security restricts every table to
# the signed-in user's own rows.
# Leave both lines commented for the default in-memory mode.
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
# VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_your_key_here

# Server half. THE SECRET KEY BYPASSES ROW-LEVEL SECURITY.
# Never prefix it with VITE_, never paste it into client code, and never commit
# it — this repository is public (docs/DECISIONS.md D25). Two tests enforce that
# mechanically rather than by intention: apps/client/test/build.test.ts asserts
# no secret-key value or server-only variable name reaches the production
# bundle, and apps/client/test/architecture.test.ts asserts no client source
# file can even name one.
#
# The two must be set together, and the URL must be the project's https URL (a
# stray character here fails at startup rather than deep inside the SDK).
# Leave both lines commented for the default in-memory mode.
# SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_your_key_here
